# Broker access control list — principle of least privilege.
#
# Threat model: assume ONE device will be compromised (stale firmware, key
# extracted from flash, unit stolen from the garden). The ACL keeps the blast
# radius inside that device: it cannot forge someone else's telemetry, cannot
# issue commands, and cannot read the household's traffic.
#
# %u is replaced with the username, %c with the client id.

# --- Hub ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The hub is the only principal with broad access: it subscribes to all
# telemetry and is the sole publisher of commands, which is what guarantees the
# arbitration and failsafe rules from ADR-0006 cannot be bypassed.
user smarthome-hub
topic readwrite home/#
topic read $SYS/broker/uptime

# --- Devices ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every device gets its own username in the form dev-{deviceID}. The patterns
# below are the template applied during provisioning (SH-002).
#
# `pattern` expands %u, so a single rule covers all devices without the file
# growing with each new one.

# A device publishes its telemetry and state — only under its own path.
pattern write home/+/dev/%u/telemetry
pattern write home/+/dev/%u/state
pattern write home/+/dev/%u/cmd/ack

# A device subscribes only to its own commands. It cannot read anyone else's.
pattern read home/+/dev/%u/cmd

# Note: no rule grants broad read access to any device. That is intentional.
